{"description":"CDC updates its global Level 2 polio travel advisory to add Laos and Namibia, remove Finland, Ghana, Spain and Zimbabwe, and urges travelers to stay up‑to‑date on polio vaccination.","articleSummary":"The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) revised its global Level 2 polio travel advisory on March 17, 2026, adding Laos and Namibia to the list of countries with detected poliovirus in the last 13 months while removing Finland, Ghana, Spain and Zimbabwe.  The advisory, which covers more than two dozen countries—including the United Kingdom and Germany—advises all travelers to ensure their routine polio vaccination series is complete and recommends that adults who have not yet received an adult booster consider an inactivated polio vaccine (IPV) booster.  The CDC also outlines a three‑dose schedule for adults who have never been vaccinated: first dose at any time, second dose one to two months later, and third dose six to 12 months later.

The article explains that polio is a highly contagious virus that may cause serious complications such as paralysis by attacking nerve cells in the brain and spinal cord.  Symptoms can include fever, fatigue, nausea, headache, nasal congestion, sore throat, cough, neck and back stiffness, and arm or leg pain, with paralysis occurring rarely.  No antiviral treatment exists once infection has begun; care focuses on symptom control, according to Harvard Medical School.  The CDC’s Level 2 advisories encourage travelers to practice enhanced precautions, whereas Level 1 advisories are more general and Level 3–4 advisories would advise against all travel.

In addition to polio, the CDC has current travel advisories for rabies in India, chikungunya, measles, and other diseases, but no Level 3 or Level 4 notifications are active at the time of publication.","points":["The CDC’s Level 2 polio travel advisory now includes Laos and Namibia and excludes Finland, Ghana, Spain and Zimbabwe.","The advisory applies to more than two dozen countries, including the United Kingdom and Germany.","The CDC recommends all travelers be up‑to‑date on routine polio vaccination and suggests adult boosters of inactivated polio vaccine if a booster has not yet been received.","Adults who have never been vaccinated should receive three polio doses spaced 1–2 months, then 6–12 months later.","Polio can cause paralysis by attacking nerve cells; treatment is supportive and no drug can kill the virus after infection begins.","The CDC’s Level 2 advisories encourage enhanced precautions, while Level 1 is less restrictive and Level 3–4 would advise against travel."],"biasSummary":"The article presents CDC recommendations and factual information without overt editorializing.  It maintains a neutral tone, primarily citing CDC statements and Harvard Medical School remarks.  The piece subtly promotes vaccination compliance but does not contain discernible political bias."}
